Hi @phuongncn, thanks for sharing this! Many of your findings are surprisingly consistent with my experiments.

I started from a relatively simple Duck + Qwen setup with a public LB around 1.65. One of the more reproducible submissions later reached 2.86 (now its 3.48 after around 3x resubmit, not reproducible as I thought, I guess😅). Here are the main things I tried and learned.

### 1. Reproducible serving mattered more than expected

Before changing the agent logic, I worked on making the inference stack deterministic and auditable:

- exact model and runtime profile;
- pinned serving configuration;
- MTP and concurrency configuration;
- startup validation;
- real tool-call preflight;
- fail-closed behavior when the wrong model or parser is loaded.

This produced a more reliable improvement than several complicated prompting experiments. A sophisticated agent is difficult to evaluate when serving behavior changes between runs.

### 2. Unconditional recovery usually made things worse

My earlier recovery agents were too eager to intervene. They often:

- retried actions that had already failed;
- changed hypotheses too frequently;
- entered long loops;
- consumed many tokens without producing level progress.

More recovery was not automatically better recovery.

What worked better was single-step verified recovery:

1. propose only one recovery action;
2. execute it;
3. observe the actual state transition;
4. continue only when there is evidence that the action helped;
5. otherwise apply a cooldown and return control to the normal policy.

I later added route gating so recovery is triggered by observable symptoms such as repeated no-op transitions, persistent stagnation, or a failed action pattern—not merely because the model says it is uncertain.

### 3. Memory helped only when it stored verified facts

Free-form summaries can preserve an incorrect theory for a very long time.

I changed the memory system to prefer verified transition facts such as:

- action X changed object Y;
- action X was a no-op in state Z;
- progress occurred after a particular interaction;
- a hypothesis was contradicted by the next observation.

Memory is scoped by game/level and can be invalidated. I use retrieved strategies as hypotheses, not instructions.

This reduced some repeated mistakes, although memory contamination is still a real risk.

### 4. Multi-role reasoning was useful only when gated

I also experimented with separating planning, verification, recovery, and action selection.

I agree with your conclusion: running every role on every step is too expensive. It can also create “analysis loops” where agents debate while the environment receives very few useful actions.

My current direction is closer to:

- one primary actor for normal play;
- a verifier only for risky or ambiguous actions;
- a recovery specialist only after measurable stagnation;
- a cooldown before the same specialist can be invoked again.

So it is a conditional mixture of roles rather than a fixed multi-agent pipeline.

### 5. Adaptive compute allocation looks more promising than a fixed action budget

I tested a progress-weighted allocator in shadow mode. It estimates useful action costs from completed levels, grants additional budget after real progress, and proposes stopping only after persistent stagnation.

On my 25-game diagnostic run it produced:

- 4 proposed early stops;
- 0 observed cases with later progress after those proposals;
- 489 actions after the proposed stopping points;
- approximately 13.6% counterfactual action savings.

This is still a very small public sample, so I do not consider it proof of private-set generalization. I am now testing the policy in enforcement mode while keeping the model, prompt, sampling, and serving profile unchanged.

### 6. Public-game-specific routing was tempting but dangerous

Per-game rules can improve a local benchmark quickly, but they are likely to fail on semi-private and private games.

I am trying to replace game IDs and manually recognized game types with generic signals:

- state-change rate;
- repeated no-op count;
- progress recency;
- action diversity;
- hypothesis survival;
- recovery success rate;
- remaining compute.

The goal is to learn when to explore, exploit, verify, recover, or stop without knowing the game identity.

### 7. “RL” was more useful as online control than as immediate weight training

I considered full reinforcement learning, but training a large model inside this workflow is expensive and difficult to validate.

The more practical version so far resembles a contextual bandit or lightweight online controller:

- reward verified progress;
- penalize no-op loops and repeated failed actions;
- allocate more compute to routes that recently produced progress;
- reduce budget for repeatedly unsuccessful recovery routes;
- never update from an unverified self-reported success.

There are no gradient updates during the Kaggle run. The learned component controls inference-time decisions and compute allocation.

### 8. I am now testing a second model, but as a controlled ablation

I am preparing a Nemotron Nano 30B-A3B FP8 experiment while keeping the surrounding harness as fixed as possible.

Because this checkpoint is text-only, it will not test visual recognition directly. It will test whether a different model improves:

- planning;
- Python/tool use;
- hypothesis revision;
- structured action generation.

The existing Qwen configuration remains the control.

### My biggest overall lesson

The best-performing direction has not been “let the model think more.” It has been:

**act → observe → verify → update memory → allocate compute based on evidence.**

Complexity helped only when it was conditional and grounded in actual environment transitions.

I am especially curious about two details from your experiments:

1. Did your manually played training data contain failed attempts and recovery trajectories, or mostly successful demonstrations?
2. For the multi-role setup, did every role receive the full history, or did each role get a compressed role-specific state?

Those two choices may explain why imitation learning and role splitting become expensive without consistently improving gameplay.
